      "MISSION 1: Mythical Miniature Retrieval\nCategory: Infiltration & Espionage\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nRetrieve a stolen miniature of Mount Hymettus with supernatural properties from a\nprivate collector's secured villa in Voula. The miniature, created by a genius artist\nliving in Ilioupoli, induces hallucinations tied to Ancient Greek mythology and\ntragedies, causing physical harm to those affected. Infiltrate the villa, bypass its\nadvanced security, and recover the miniature. Return it to the artist's house near\nMount Hymettus in Ilioupoli, where he plans to store it in a secure and undisclosed\nlocation to prevent future theft.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe Voula villa has a secured gate, front garden and main hall leading to the collector's salon. A security panel beside the salon doorway controls an electronically lockable door at the head of the main basement stair. That stair descends into a climate-controlled basement gallery, which opens directly to a small vault room and an equipment alcove. A separate service stair from the kitchen reaches the equipment alcove, which opens directly back into the gallery; the service-stair door uses a separate mechanical latch and is closed but unlocked at mission start. The vault room has one door and no hidden exit. The pool terrace remains upstairs. The artist waits at the separate Ilioupoli destination in a prepared containment room.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe collector remains in the salon with one guest, beside the doorway leading to the hall security panel. One security guard covers the front hall while a second is due to begin the next basement round in six minutes. A conservator works in the equipment alcove and is frightened by the object but follows the collector's orders. The artist waits at the Ilioupoli destination.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe miniature is locked inside a transparent climate case in the basement vault after repeated incidents upstairs. The vault door and climate case each have physical contact sensors routed to the equipment-alcove panel, which also monitors case temperature; opening either secured contact without its authorized key or code, or pushing case temperature outside tolerance, raises the alarm. The case reduces but does not eliminate the miniature's effect. Inside the basement, the dominant hallucination makes straight corridors feel like branching passages and can cause people to strike walls or each other while believing they are in a labyrinth. The physical basement remains exactly the stated two-stair, one-vault layout.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success opens or removes the climate case, maintains physical orientation, extracts the miniature and seals it inside the artist's containment room in Ilioupoli. Partial success delivers the miniature but only after prolonged uncontained exposure causes a serious injury. Failure leaves the miniature secured in the collector-controlled vault after all credible access is lost, or leaves the player captured, incapacitated or otherwise unable to complete delivery.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack the six minutes until the basement guard's round, conservator position, vault code, climate-case key, exposure and orientation anchors. The basement remains the same gallery, equipment alcove, two stairs and single-door vault even when extra passages are perceived.\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe miniature is visible inside the climate case in the basement vault. The conservator is working in the equipment alcove. The next basement guard round begins in six minutes. Chalk maintenance marks on the real walls provide stable orientation when the hallucination begins.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The miniature starts inside the transparent climate case in the single-door vault room.\n- The conservator wears the climate-case key on a belt clip.\n- The collector retains the vault-door code upstairs.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nBypass the villa security and basement containment, recover the miniature without surrendering to false spatial cues, and return it to the artist.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nSmall chalk maintenance numbers remain consistent even when the basement seems to branch into impossible corridors.\n\nLive danger:\nLabyrinth hallucinations can turn ordinary walls, stairs and other people into immediate physical hazards when someone acts on false geometry.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A bronze bull sculpture in the gallery is an ordinary artwork despite becoming prominent in hallucinations.\n- The equipment-alcove panel monitors physical door/case contacts and temperature, not a supernatural containment field.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe service stair's metal handrail remains continuous and can serve as a physical orientation anchor when the basement seems to branch.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- In six minutes, the basement guard descends the main stair through the stair door and checks the vault door.\n- If the vault alarm is triggered, the collector steps to the security panel beside the salon doorway and locks the main-stair door. The service-stair door remains closed but unlocked.\n- Sealing the miniature in the artist's containment room ends the active labyrinth effect.\n\n",
