      "MISSION 1: Syntagma Campaign Sabotage\nCategory: Infiltration & Espionage\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nSabotage a corrupt politician's campaign headquarters near Syntagma.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nStreet -> campaign reception -> volunteer bullpen -> press-preparation room. The press room connects the print room and candidate corridor; the candidate corridor reaches the senior aide office and rear staff exit. Twenty numbered press-kit shells occupy shelves beside the print room, and a departure stack stands beside the candidate-corridor doorway.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nVolunteers assemble press kits. The research director carries the locked source-USB case from the senior aide office toward the press-preparation room. The senior aide remains in the office. The print operator works in the print room, while the press aide stands at the numbered shelves with the distribution checklist and rolling crate. Journalists remain outside until the scheduled briefing.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe corrupt campaign is about to distribute a knowingly falsified smear package. The run contains exactly twenty numbered outgoing kits. Each complete kit requires one final dossier insert and one of the twenty source USBs in the research director's locked case. The print operator controls the twenty-insert final run; the research director controls the USB case. A kit becomes eligible for departure only after both components are inserted and the press aide checks its number on the distribution list.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success leaves no usable genuine smear kit available for the scheduled briefing by contaminating, replacing or disabling the outgoing kits or their authoritative components, without exposing the method before it takes effect. Partial success compromises only part of the twenty-kit run or secures proof of fabrication while at least one clean kit is delivered. Failure occurs when all twenty clean kits pass to journalists beyond credible recovery with no proof secured, or when the operation is exposed before any meaningful sabotage or evidence gain.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack the twenty-insert print run, source-USB case, numbered kit completion, checklist and briefing departures. Twelve final inserts are already printed and the remaining eight are still printing. The research director is already carrying the locked USB case toward the numbered shelves. Once there, the director personally inserts one source USB into each kit while a volunteer adds the matching final paper insert and the press aide checks the number. Assembly proceeds one kit at a time in numerical order. At the scheduled briefing, the press aide makes a first trip with up to ten checked kits and later returns for up to ten remaining checked kits.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe smear operation is physically embedded in an otherwise normal campaign press room. Twelve of the twenty final inserts have printed and the thirteenth is running. No kit is complete yet. The research director is halfway along the candidate corridor carrying the locked case of twenty source USBs toward the numbered shelves; the press aide waits there with the checklist and rolling crate.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The authoritative paper dossier is the twenty-insert final print run; twelve inserts are already on the print-room output stack and eight remain to print.\n- Twenty authoritative source USBs start in the research director's locked case.\n- The twenty numbered outgoing press kits become complete only when both components are inserted and the press aide checks off each number.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nPrevent the twenty genuine outgoing smear kits from reaching the scheduled briefing as usable complete packages.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe numbered shelf and distribution checklist separate the true outgoing kits from piles of ordinary campaign material; the research director personally inserts the source USBs while volunteers handle the paper inserts and kit shells.\n\nLive danger:\nAt the scheduled briefing, the press aide can carry up to ten checked kits through the candidate corridor and rear staff exit; after returning with the rolling crate, the aide can carry up to ten remaining checked kits. Clean kits already handed to journalists are separate from those still inside.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A dramatic draft dossier on a volunteer desk contains placeholder text and is not scheduled for release.\n- Boxes marked PRESS near reception contain bottled water and badges for the briefing.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe twelve completed final inserts lie face down in print order on the output table while the thirteenth is running. Their edges are still warm from the printer.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When the research director reaches the numbered shelves, the director unlocks the USB case and begins the kit-by-kit assembly sequence with the volunteer and press aide.\n- As the remaining print run completes, the twentieth final insert joins the output stack.\n- Each completed numbered kit receives one source USB and one final insert, is checked off by the press aide and moves to the departure stack beside the candidate-corridor doorway.\n- At the scheduled briefing, the press aide loads up to ten checked kits into the rolling crate, takes candidate corridor -> rear staff exit and hands those kits to the waiting journalists. After returning with the rolling crate, the aide repeats the route with up to ten remaining checked kits; there is no third scheduled briefing trip.",
