      "MISSION 1: Rescue an Australian Yacht Owner in Alimos\nCategory: Rescue Missions\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nSave an Australian yacht owner from pirates holding him hostage in the Alimos\nmarina.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe yacht is temporarily moored at the marina fuel dock near the basin exit:\nfuel office -> quay -> fuel berth -> yacht deck. The salon stairs lead to an\naft cabin where the owner is held. The fuel dock has open public sightlines\nand a vehicle lane behind the office; the yacht can leave directly toward the\nharbor mouth once lines are cast off.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nA pirate leader waits on deck for a digital ransom confirmation; a second\npirate guards the owner below; a third remains in a car behind the fuel office\nas road lookout. Fuel staff are present but have been told the owner is ill\nand wants privacy.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe yacht is fully fueled and mechanically ready. The pirates intend to depart\nimmediately after the ransom clears. The road lookout can warn the deck but\ncannot reach the hostage without crossing the quay. Fuel staff become useful\nwitnesses only if given a concrete reason to doubt the cover story.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success frees the owner before the yacht clears the marina and prevents\nthe road lookout escaping by car during the extraction. Partial success frees\nhim after the yacht has cast off but before it clears the marina, or stops the\nboat while the road lookout escapes by car. Failure lets the yacht leave the\nbasin with the owner.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack ransom confirmation, mooring lines, engine state, road-lookout warning\nand owner cabin. The engine is already warm; the decisive clock is the\nconfirmed payment, not arbitrary elapsed turns.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nFueling has finished and the crew is lingering only for the ransom message.\nThe owner is bound in the aft cabin while the deck pirate watches the phone\nand the quay.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The owner starts in the aft cabin.\n- The yacht starts fueled, engine warm and held by two mooring lines at the\nfuel berth.\n- The pirate leader starts on deck with the ransom phone; the road lookout\nstarts in the parked car behind the fuel office.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRemove the owner from pirate custody before the payment confirmation turns the\nfueled yacht into a departing hostage vehicle.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nFuel staff confirm that the yacht finished fueling several minutes ago but the\nsupposed owner has not appeared on deck once.\n\nLive danger:\nPayment confirmation causes immediate line release and departure toward the\nharbor mouth.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A second car waiting near the fuel office belongs to a customer paying an\nordinary marina account.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nA fuel employee coils the delivery hose away from the yacht before payment\nconfirmation, leaving the mooring lines as the remaining physical restraint.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When payment confirmation arrives, the deck pirate releases the stern line\nand then the bow line from the yacht side; the cabin guard brings the owner up\nonly if departure is obstructed.\n- If the road lookout sees an obvious armed approach, the lookout calls the\ndeck pirate and drives toward the marina gate.\n- Once both mooring lines are released and the helm remains under pirate\ncontrol, the yacht begins moving toward the basin exit.",
