      "MISSION 1: Kallithea Precinct Evidence Burn\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nDestroy evidence in a police precinct in Kallithea.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nPublic entrance -> front desk -> investigations corridor -> detective office -> interview-room branch. The detective office contains a locked court-file cabinet. The same corridor reaches a rear report room and staff exit to the vehicle yard. The designated prosecutor-run patrol car is parked in the yard beside a gated street exit. The property room is elsewhere in the precinct and no longer holds the target items because they have been formally signed out.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nA case detective has custody of the court bundle and moves between the detective office and an interview room. A junior officer prepares the vehicle log in the report room. The front-desk sergeant controls visitors. A patrol driver reports to the designated car in the yard before the scheduled prosecutor run. No property clerk retains custody of the target evidence.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe target consists of an original witness audio recorder and its signed paper transcript, both already signed out into the detective's court-file cabinet. The transcript alone is contestable without the recorder, and the recorder alone lacks the signed identification chain; both must be destroyed to remove the intended evidence package.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success irreversibly destroys both recorder and signed transcript before the court bundle leaves the precinct. Partial success destroys one or creates an obvious attributable breach. Failure lets either substantive item leave the precinct in the patrol car or remain recoverable.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack detective movement, cabinet custody, interview duration, vehicle-log completion, patrol-driver arrival and the court bundle's movement toward the rear yard.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe evidence has already moved out of property storage for morning court preparation. The detective is conducting one final witness clarification before placing the recorder and transcript into a transport pouch.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The original witness recorder and signed transcript start in the detective's locked court-file cabinet.\n- The detective alone has the cabinet key during the interview sequence.\n- After the final clarification, both items go into the court transport pouch for the rear-yard run.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nDestroy the original recorder and signed transcript before the patrol car carries them out of the precinct.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe vehicle log in the rear report room gives the exact patrol car and departure sequence for the prosecutor run.\n\nLive danger:\nThe detective repeatedly returns to the office between interview segments and notices any disturbed court-file arrangement.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- Photocopies of unrelated statements in the report room are not part of the target bundle.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe detective leaves the office for a five-minute interview-room segment while the junior officer remains in the report room filling the vehicle log.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When the final witness clarification ends, the detective returns to the cabinet and prepares the transport pouch.\n- When the patrol driver signs the vehicle log and receives the pouch, it is carried to the designated patrol car; the evidence remains inside the precinct until that car clears the yard gate.\n- Destroying both substantive items before the patrol car clears the yard gate completes the evidence-destruction objective even if administrative records still show that they once existed.",
