      "MISSION 1: Maniatika Family Feud\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nRaid a stronghold in the Maniatika area of Piraeus, where a family feud is\nescalating into open warfare.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe stronghold is a large family apartment opening onto a roofed courtyard behind a street-level metal gate. The courtyard connects the kitchen, a front sitting room and a narrow stair to two upstairs bedrooms. A covered storage bay at the back holds chairs, tools and several long-gun cases. The only street exit is the metal gate; an upper balcony overlooks the courtyard but not the full street.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nA wake for a recently killed uncle fills the kitchen and sitting room with relatives, including children and older family members. Four younger armed men occupy the courtyard and storage bay. The dead man's son is preparing a retaliatory shooting with two cousins. The widow and an older sister know the uncle had arranged a private meeting with the rival branch before his death and oppose retaliation.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe dead uncle had already opened a truce channel; a folded note naming the meeting and its witness remains inside his jacket in the upstairs bedroom. The named witness is a real mutual family acquaintance who can corroborate the arrangement. The son believes the note is sentimental and has not read it. He plans to leave with two gun cases after the last outside mourners depart. The widow will reveal the note only when approached without humiliating the son in front of the wake.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success prevents the retaliatory departure and establishes the dead uncle's own truce effort strongly enough to fracture support for the shooting. Partial success stops the departure by force while leaving the feud intact. Failure sends the son and cousins into the street armed or turns the crowded wake into a firefight.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack mourner departures, gun-case custody, the son's status among younger relatives and the widow's willingness to speak. The crowded wake makes indiscriminate force unusually costly and gives the son social cover until he starts moving the cases.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe family is mourning one of the feud's latest dead while the dead man's son treats the wake as the last gathering before revenge. Most people inside do not know that the deceased had been trying to arrange a quiet truce meeting.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The dead uncle's truce note starts inside the inner pocket of his jacket in the upstairs bedroom.\n- Two loaded gun cases start in the covered storage bay under the son's control.\n- The son intends to leave through the metal gate after the final outside mourners depart.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nPrevent the wake from becoming the launch point for another killing and secure the dead uncle's truce evidence or equivalent leverage before the armed group leaves.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe widow reacts sharply when relatives describe the dead man as having wanted revenge, then looks toward the upstairs bedroom.\n\nLive danger:\nThe courtyard is full of relatives who can be trapped between armed young men and the street gate.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A sealed envelope of funeral cash in the kitchen has no connection to the feud's financing.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nCondolence visitors leave coats along the stair rail, narrowing the upstairs approach without closing it.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When the last outside mourners leave, the son lifts the first gun case from the storage bay and calls the cousins to the gate.\n- When the widow sees the son publicly shamed or attacked, she stops volunteering the truce note's meaning.\n- When the truce note is authenticated through the named witness, several older relatives withdraw support from the retaliation.",
