      "MISSION 1: Gero-Thanassis' Secret Recordings Heist\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nA wealthy music collector has caught wind of a legendary, hidden collection: an\narchive of unreleased 60s and 70s recordings produced by the elusive \"gero-Thanassis,\"\nfeaturing some of the era's most iconic voices and composers. Held in gero-Thanassis'\nold house in Perama, this collection is fiercely guarded by his two great-grandsons,\nTrap artists with little interest in their ancestor's legacy but resolute in honoring\nhis last wish to keep the recordings secret. Your client wants these records at any\ncost, knowing they'll never willingly sell. Your task: infiltrate the Perama home,\navoid (or neutralize) the great-grandsons' suspicions, and retrieve as much of the\ncollection as possible. Deliver the music to the collector's sleek apartment near\nFilopappou without a trace, securing a trove of timeless songs that was almost lost to\nobscurity.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nStreet -> front room -> hall -> ancestor bedroom and kitchen. A narrow kitchen passage reaches a rear yard and an exterior utility stair to the flat roof. Roof repair has opened a low ceiling hatch above the ancestor bedroom; a short ladder from the bedroom reaches the cavity. Damp storage remains closed behind the hall and contains no target material. Delivery still ends at Filopappou.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nOne great-grandson supervises a roofer who works between rear yard and roof; the second is in the trap-studio room off the front room. The roofer is an ordinary tradesman and does not know about the archive. Both grandsons know that the ancestor's tapes are hidden in the ceiling cavity and intend to close it again as soon as the repair is finished.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nEight master reels are packed in a shallow metal case inside the ceiling cavity above the ancestor bedroom. The case was exposed when damp plaster was removed. When the roofer completes the patch, one grandson carries the ladder out and screws a fresh board over the hatch; the case stays above the board unless someone removes it first.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires the eight-reel case to reach the Filopappou client intact and untraced. Partial success covers authentic reels delivered with damage or a clear witness trail. Finishing the repair is not itself Failure: the case remains in the cavity behind the new board. Failure requires destruction or loss beyond recovery, the grandsons removing and securing the case so that no credible route remains, or loss of the recovered case before delivery.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack the roofer's finite work, access to the bedroom ladder, the open ceiling hatch, plaster dust as trace, and the case's weight. The repair closes the easy access because the work is actually completed, not because of elapsed narrative turns.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nA mundane roof repair has temporarily exposed the archive's real hiding place. The grandsons are more worried about damp reaching the tapes than about thieves, creating a narrow practical opening without weakening their refusal to let the recordings leave.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- Target: one shallow metal case containing eight labeled master reels in the open ceiling cavity above the ancestor bedroom.\n- The bedroom ladder is the only ready access to the cavity at the start.\n- Fresh plaster and screws will close the hatch when the roofer finishes.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRemove the authentic reel case from the ceiling cavity and deliver it intact to Filopappou without leaving a trace that reliably identifies the player or client.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nFresh plaster dust on the ancestor-bedroom floor stops directly below a low ceiling hatch that looks older than the current repair.\n\nLive danger:\nThe ordinary roofer repeatedly crosses the rear yard and bedroom route while one grandson supervises the work.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- Damp storage contains empty reel boxes kept for sentimental reasons.\n- The roofer's tool case contains only trade tools and receipts.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe roofer is on the roof and the supervising grandson stands in the rear yard below him. The second grandson is in the studio, leaving the ancestor-bedroom ladder unused but the yard route observed.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When the repair is completed, the ladder leaves the bedroom and the hatch is boarded shut; the case remains in the cavity, while reopening the fresh work leaves conspicuous screw and plaster disturbance.\n- Removing the case without controlling plaster and screw traces gives the grandsons a clear reconstruction of the theft.",
