      "MISSION 1: High-Profile Safehouse\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nFind and raid a high-profile criminal's safehouse in Gerakas, filled with\nstolen goods and cash. Avoid traps and ambushes set by the criminal's\nhenchmen.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nA modern two-level maisonette on a quiet Gerakas street: small front court ->\nground-floor salon and kitchen -> internal stair -> upper landing with office\nand bedroom. A side carport connects to a rear gate. Stolen watches and phones\nare stored in lockboxes under the stair; cash and the criminal's black account\nnotebook are in the upstairs office.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe principal criminal is inside the upstairs office. One henchman is in the\nground-floor salon and a driver waits by a car in the side carport. A second henchman is\nabsent on an errand and has a fixed return time. The driver leaves only with a\ndirect order or concrete alarm.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe black account notebook is the decisive network evidence and begins in the\nupstairs office desk. The stolen lockboxes are genuine. A pressure mat on the\nupper landing rings a local buzzer in the office if crossed without stepping\naround its visible edge; it does not summon outside forces by itself.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires securing the black account notebook and at least one\nidentifiable stolen-goods lockbox or a clear record of its contents, then\nleaving the property. Capturing the principal adds value but is not required.\nPartial success gets only property or only the notebook. Failure secures\nneither side of the objective, or ends with the player trapped before\nextracting what was secured.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack principal position, ground-floor henchman, driver state, pressure-mat\nbuzzer, returning henchman and the front/rear exits. The small house makes\nvertical movement and noise more important than an elaborate trap network.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe safehouse is actively occupied. The most valuable-looking goods are under\nthe stair, but the black account notebook upstairs ties those goods to named\nbuyers and dates.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The black account notebook begins in the upstairs office desk under the\n  principal's control.\n- Identifiable stolen watches and phones begin in lockboxes beneath the\n  internal stair.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRaid the occupied maisonette, secure the network notebook plus attributable\nstolen property, and escape before the defenders close both exits.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA thin cable from the upper-landing pressure mat runs openly beneath the\noffice door, making the local alarm visible to careful observation.\n\nLive danger:\nThe principal is armed upstairs while a henchman controls the ground floor, so\ncareless vertical movement can put the player between them.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A bedroom wall safe holds personal documents and legal jewelry.\n- The driver's car contains no stolen-goods ledger.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe principal has stepped from the office into the upper bedroom while the\nblack notebook remains in the desk. The ground-floor henchman is near the\nstair and the returning henchman is still off-site.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- At the fixed return time the absent henchman approaches through the front\n  street and enters only if the front route remains usable.\n- If a clear alarm reaches the driver, the driver starts the car and\n  prepares the rear gate for the principal's escape.\n",
      "MISSION 1: High-Profile Safehouse\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nFind and raid a high-profile criminal's safehouse in Gerakas, filled with\nstolen goods and cash. Avoid traps and ambushes set by the criminal's\nhenchmen.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nA closed small wholesale showroom on the Gerakas commercial fringe: street\nshutter and side personnel door -> display floor -> rear stock room -> narrow\nstair to a mezzanine office. A loading yard behind the stock room has one\nsliding gate to the service street. Stolen electronics fill part of the stock\nroom; cash and dispatch records are kept upstairs.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nTwo henchmen occupy the property: one on the display floor and one in the rear\nstock room. The principal is absent. A lookout sits in a parked car on the\nservice street with a view of the loading gate. No legitimate staff are\nexpected before morning.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe decisive dispatch ledger begins in the mezzanine office and links stolen\nserial numbers to buyers. The stock-room electronics are genuine stolen goods.\nThe display-floor alarm beam is visible at ankle height in the dark and\ntriggers a local siren when crossed; it does not silently create new\nattackers.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires securing the dispatch ledger and enough serial-number\nevidence from the stock room to identify the stolen inventory, then\nextracting. Partial success secures only one side of that proof. Failure\nleaves the records and goods under criminal control or traps the player\ninside.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack henchman positions, loading-gate state, lookout awareness, alarm-beam\nstate and whether the rear stock room remains accessible. The building has two\nexterior approaches but no hidden tunnel.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe safehouse uses a shuttered wholesale showroom as cover. The visible\ndisplay stock is cheap legal merchandise; the rear stock room contains the\nstolen electronics, and the mezzanine ledger records their dispatch.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The dispatch ledger begins in the mezzanine office.\n- The rear stock room contains stolen electronics with serial numbers\n  matching the ledger.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRaid the property, secure the dispatch ledger and matching stolen-goods proof,\nand leave through an established exit.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nSeveral rear-stock serial labels have been copied by hand onto a notepad\nvisible through the mezzanine-office glass.\n\nLive danger:\nThe two henchmen can control the narrow stair and stock-room doorway from\ndifferent levels if alerted together.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The front display stock is legal and mostly low-value.\n- A locked toilet beside the personnel door contains no route or evidence.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe display-floor henchman is near the side personnel door, the stock-room\nhenchman is at the loading bench, and the lookout remains in the\nservice-street car. The sliding loading gate is closed.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the local siren sounds, the service-street lookout approaches the\n  loading gate and warns the two henchmen by phone.\n- If alerted, a henchman who is in the loading yard can close the sliding\n  gate from there; the street personnel door remains a separate exit.\n",
