      "MISSION 1: High-Profile Safehouse\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nFind and raid a high-profile criminal's safehouse in Gerakas, filled with\nstolen goods and cash. Avoid traps and ambushes set by the criminal's\nhenchmen.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nA first-floor safehouse above a shuttered home-improvement showroom. The side\nstreet door opens at a ground-floor stair foot; from there, a narrow stair\nreaches the apartment hall, while a short rear passage reaches a locked stock\ncage behind the showroom. The hall reaches a living room used for counting\ncash, a back office, kitchen and balcony. The stock cage holds stolen power\ntools; the stair/side-door route is the only internal connection to the\nstreet. The balcony overlooks a rear yard but has no neighboring bridge or\nfire stair.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe principal criminal is absent. One henchman counts cash in the living room\nand carries the stock-cage key; another uses the back office. A street lookout\nalternates between the side-door view and the rear yard from outside. The\nground-floor stock cage is currently locked and unoccupied.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe back-office laptop contains the stolen-goods inventory and buyer list. The\ncash in the living room is genuine but cannot substitute for the laptop\nevidence. The stolen tools remain in the ground-floor cage until someone physically\nunlocks and moves them. The simple contact alarm on the stock-cage door is\narmed at mission start and rings upstairs if the door is opened before the\nalarm is disabled.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires securing the inventory laptop and proving or securing\nthe matching stolen stock downstairs, then extracting. Partial success gets\nonly the laptop or only the goods. Failure secures neither side of the\nobjective, or ends with the player trapped before extracting what was\nsecured.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack two upstairs henchmen, lookout position, laptop custody, stock-cage\nalarm and the single apartment stair. The balcony gives observation but not an\nescape route.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe upstairs rooms look like a cash-counting flat, while the actual stolen\ngoods are one level below behind the shuttered showroom. The back-office\nlaptop ties the two spaces together through serial numbers and buyer names.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The inventory laptop begins on the back-office desk.\n- Matching stolen power tools begin locked inside the ground-floor stock\n  cage.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRaid the two-level safehouse, secure the inventory laptop and matching\nstolen-goods proof, and leave without being trapped on the single stair.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA printed serial-number sheet beside the upstairs laptop matches labels\nvisible through the ground-floor stock-cage mesh.\n\nLive danger:\nAn alerted pair of henchmen can control the apartment stair while the exterior\nlookout watches the side-door exit.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The living-room cash bundles are genuine proceeds but contain no buyer\n  list.\n- The showroom floor contains only old legal display units.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nOne henchman is counting cash in the living room, the other is in the back\noffice with the laptop, and the lookout is outside facing the side door.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the stock-cage contact alarm rings, both upstairs henchmen know someone\n  is downstairs and move toward the apartment stair according to their\n  current positions.\n- If the laptop is physically removed from the office, its custody remains\n  with whoever carries it; no remote wipe begins without another established\n  cause.\n",
      "MISSION 1: High-Profile Safehouse\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nFind and raid a high-profile criminal's safehouse in Gerakas, filled with\nstolen goods and cash. Avoid traps and ambushes set by the criminal's\nhenchmen.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nA first-floor safehouse above a shuttered home-improvement showroom. The side\nstreet door opens at a ground-floor stair foot; from there, a narrow stair\nreaches the apartment hall, while a short rear passage reaches a locked stock\ncage behind the showroom. The hall reaches a living room used for counting\ncash, a back office, kitchen and balcony. The stock cage holds stolen power\ntools; the stair/side-door route is the only internal connection to the\nstreet. The balcony overlooks a rear yard but has no neighboring bridge or\nfire stair.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe principal criminal is absent. One henchman counts cash in the living room\nand carries the stock-cage key; another uses the back office. A street lookout\nalternates between the side-door view and the rear yard from outside. The\nground-floor stock cage is currently locked and unoccupied.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe back-office laptop contains the stolen-goods inventory and buyer list. The\ncash in the living room is genuine but cannot substitute for the laptop\nevidence. The stolen tools remain in the ground-floor cage until someone physically\nunlocks and moves them. The simple contact alarm on the stock-cage door is\narmed at mission start and rings upstairs if the door is opened before the\nalarm is disabled.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires securing the inventory laptop and proving or securing\nthe matching stolen stock downstairs, then extracting. Partial success gets\nonly the laptop or only the goods. Failure secures neither side of the\nobjective, or ends with the player trapped before extracting what was\nsecured.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack two upstairs henchmen, lookout position, laptop custody, stock-cage\nalarm and the single apartment stair. The balcony gives observation but not an\nescape route.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe upstairs rooms look like a cash-counting flat, while the actual stolen\ngoods are one level below behind the shuttered showroom. The back-office\nlaptop ties the two spaces together through serial numbers and buyer names.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The inventory laptop begins on the back-office desk.\n- Matching stolen power tools begin locked inside the ground-floor stock\n  cage.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRaid the two-level safehouse, secure the inventory laptop and matching\nstolen-goods proof, and leave without being trapped on the single stair.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA printed serial-number sheet beside the upstairs laptop matches labels\nvisible through the ground-floor stock-cage mesh.\n\nLive danger:\nAn alerted pair of henchmen can control the apartment stair while the exterior\nlookout watches the side-door exit.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The living-room cash bundles are genuine proceeds but contain no buyer\n  list.\n- The showroom floor contains only old legal display units.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe back-office henchman has gone to the kitchen, leaving the laptop on the\ndesk. The cash counter remains in the living room and the lookout has shifted\ntoward the rear yard.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the stock-cage contact alarm rings, both upstairs henchmen know someone\n  is downstairs and move toward the apartment stair according to their\n  current positions.\n- If the laptop is physically removed from the office, its custody remains\n  with whoever carries it; no remote wipe begins without another established\n  cause.\n",
      "MISSION 1: High-Profile Safehouse\nCategory: Heists & Raids\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nFind and raid a high-profile criminal's safehouse in Gerakas, filled with\nstolen goods and cash. Avoid traps and ambushes set by the criminal's\nhenchmen.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nA first-floor safehouse above a shuttered home-improvement showroom. The side\nstreet door opens at a ground-floor stair foot; from there, a narrow stair\nreaches the apartment hall, while a short rear passage reaches a locked stock\ncage behind the showroom. The hall reaches a living room used for counting\ncash, a back office, kitchen and balcony. The stock cage holds stolen power\ntools; the stair/side-door route is the only internal connection to the\nstreet. The balcony overlooks a rear yard but has no neighboring bridge or\nfire stair.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe principal criminal is absent. One henchman counts cash in the living room\nand carries the stock-cage key; another uses the back office. A street lookout\nalternates between the side-door view and the rear yard from outside. The\nground-floor stock cage is currently locked and unoccupied.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe back-office laptop contains the stolen-goods inventory and buyer list. The\ncash in the living room is genuine but cannot substitute for the laptop\nevidence. The stolen tools remain in the ground-floor cage until someone physically\nunlocks and moves them. The simple contact alarm on the stock-cage door is\narmed at mission start and rings upstairs if the door is opened before the\nalarm is disabled.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success requires securing the inventory laptop and proving or securing\nthe matching stolen stock downstairs, then extracting. Partial success gets\nonly the laptop or only the goods. Failure secures neither side of the\nobjective, or ends with the player trapped before extracting what was\nsecured.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack two upstairs henchmen, lookout position, laptop custody, stock-cage\nalarm and the single apartment stair. The balcony gives observation but not an\nescape route.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe upstairs rooms look like a cash-counting flat, while the actual stolen\ngoods are one level below behind the shuttered showroom. The back-office\nlaptop ties the two spaces together through serial numbers and buyer names.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The inventory laptop begins on the back-office desk.\n- Matching stolen power tools begin locked inside the ground-floor stock\n  cage.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nRaid the two-level safehouse, secure the inventory laptop and matching\nstolen-goods proof, and leave without being trapped on the single stair.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA printed serial-number sheet beside the upstairs laptop matches labels\nvisible through the ground-floor stock-cage mesh.\n\nLive danger:\nAn alerted pair of henchmen can control the apartment stair while the exterior\nlookout watches the side-door exit.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The living-room cash bundles are genuine proceeds but contain no buyer\n  list.\n- The showroom floor contains only old legal display units.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nBoth henchmen are in the living room reconciling cash while the back office is\nempty. The laptop remains on its desk, the ground-floor stock cage is still\nlocked, and the lookout is outside moving between the side-door view and the\nrear-yard angle.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the stock-cage contact alarm rings, both upstairs henchmen know someone\n  is downstairs and move toward the apartment stair according to their\n  current positions.\n- If the laptop is physically removed from the office, its custody remains\n  with whoever carries it; no remote wipe begins without another established\n  cause.\n"
