      "MISSION 1: Elefsina Weapons Truck Hijack\nCategory: Racing & Driving\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nHijack a truck transporting illegal weapons through Elefsina and drive it to a\nsafehouse.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe route begins at an Elefsina warehouse lane, joins a broader industrial avenue and passes a fuel stop before the freight checkpoint. Beyond the checkpoint, a service road leads directly to the safehouse turnoff. The truck can pull into the fuel stop without blocking through traffic. The escort van normally waits across the avenue during fueling and then resumes behind the truck.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe truck driver is a coerced haulier who knows the cargo is illegal but is not part of the organization. Three armed escorts ride in one van. A fuel attendant works the stop and remains uninvolved. The safehouse receiver waits at destination. The driver has a radio handset used for scheduled check-ins with the escort van.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe weapons remain in locked crates inside the truck. The driver wants out but will cooperate only if separated from the escort and given a credible chance to survive. At the fuel stop he taps the radio twice before each forced check-in. If the player gains his cooperation, he can keep the truck moving toward the safehouse; if threatened blindly, panic can disable or jackknife the vehicle.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success separates the driver from escort control, gains reliable control of the truck and reaches the safehouse with the complete weapons load aboard. Partial success gets meaningful weapons to the safehouse after the truck is abandoned or disabled before destination, or arrives with major cargo loss. Failure destroys the cargo or lets the escort regain durable control and carry the weapons truck beyond practical interception.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack fuel-stop arrival, radio check-in, driver willingness, escort-van position, checkpoint warning and vehicle condition. The driver's cooperation changes behavior, not the physical route.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe truck is approaching its scheduled fuel stop. The driver has already made one nervous radio check and keeps glancing at the escort van in the mirror.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- Weapons remain in the truck's locked cargo box.\n- The driver controls the cab and is coerced rather than loyal.\n- The escort van controls the radio relationship but does not possess the cargo.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nBreak the escort's control of the driver, take reliable control of the weapons truck and get it to the safehouse.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe driver's repeated two taps on the handset occur immediately before each escort check-in and signal his anxiety about the van.\n\nLive danger:\nA clumsy threat can make the driver panic, stall across traffic or jackknife before the player has secured the vehicle.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The fuel attendant notices nervous behavior but knows nothing about the weapons.\n- A parked tanker at the stop is unrelated to the convoy.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe fuel stop's air-and-water island leaves enough space for the truck but forces the escort van to remain across the avenue unless it abandons its normal watch position.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- At the scheduled stop, the truck pulls to the fuel lane and the escort van waits across the avenue.\n- If the driver misses the radio check-in, the escort van crosses toward the truck instead of continuing the route.\n- Once the truck passes the checkpoint without an active escort warning, the service road to the safehouse remains open unless the player's own actions block it.",
