      "MISSION 1: Elefsina Weapons Truck Hijack\nCategory: Racing & Driving\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nHijack a truck transporting illegal weapons through Elefsina and drive it to a\nsafehouse.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe truck route runs from an industrial warehouse to a fenced transshipment yard beside the old freight sidings, then from the yard toward the safehouse road. The yard has one vehicle gate, a weighbridge lane and a covered loading strip where a forklift can reach the truck's rear doors. A side pedestrian gate does not admit vehicles. The vehicle gate is the truck's only route from the yard back toward the safehouse road.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe weapons truck has one driver and one armed passenger. Two yard handlers and a forklift operator are legitimate workers coerced to unload four marked weapons crates. An escort car waits outside the yard gate. A receiver for the arms organization is due to arrive in a small panel van after unloading begins. The safehouse receiver waits separately at destination.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe truck is still transporting the illegal weapons, but the organization intends to unload the four marked weapons crates at the transshipment yard and send the truck onward with harmless cargo. Custody of each weapons crate changes only when the forklift physically removes it. Unloaded crates remain on the covered strip until physically moved again; the arriving receiver does not gain them merely by entering the yard. Hijacking before or during unloading can still satisfy the mission, but full success requires the truck to leave for the safehouse with all four weapons crates aboard or reloaded.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success takes the truck with all four weapons crates aboard and drives it to the safehouse. Partial success recovers only some crates, reaches the safehouse with an incomplete load, or secures meaningful weapons after losing the truck itself. Failure destroys the weapons, lets all four separated crates leave in the receiver's panel van beyond practical interception, or loses both the truck and any meaningful weapons recovery.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack yard-gate state, weighbridge stop, which of the four crates have been unloaded, forklift custody, escort-car position, receiver-van arrival and any reloading. Each unloaded crate remains where the forklift places it until physically moved again.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe weapons truck is approaching the transshipment yard and has not yet crossed the weighbridge. The forklift is ready under the covered strip. The escort car waits outside the yard gate.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- All four weapons crates start inside the truck.\n- The armed passenger remains in the cab until the weighbridge stop.\n- The forklift operator gains custody of one crate at a time only after rear doors open.\n- The arms receiver and panel van begin outside the yard and arrive only after unloading has begun.\n- Full mission success still requires the weapons truck to reach the safehouse.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nHijack the truck before the organization strips the weapons from it, preserve or restore the complete load and drive the truck to the safehouse.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA chalk list on the yard clipboard marks the four rear-door crates to be unloaded and shows the transfer is about to begin.\n\nLive danger:\nWaiting for a quieter moment can steadily turn one truck target into separated crates under different physical control, making complete recovery harder rather than easier.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- Unmarked pallets already in the yard contain legitimate machine parts.\n- The coerced forklift operator is not secretly part of the weapons organization.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe weighbridge printer produces a time-stamped axle-weight slip before unloading begins; the slip reflects the full weapons load at that moment.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- On yard entry, the truck stops at the weighbridge and the armed passenger gets out to supervise the rear doors.\n- Once the rear doors open, the forklift removes the four marked crates one by one to the covered strip.\n- After unloading begins, the arms receiver arrives through the vehicle gate in the panel van and waits beside the covered strip.\n- When the fourth crate is unloaded, the weapons truck is scheduled to leave stripped of weapons and the receiver orders the forklift to load the separated crates into the panel van; only actual departure beyond practical interception makes that separated load irretrievable.",
