      "MISSION 1: Saronida Cliffside Chase\nCategory: Racing & Driving\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nEngage in a high-speed chase along the coastal roads of Saronida. If the target isn't stopped in time, they will abandon their vehicle and go into hiding in a cliffside cave, where you'll need to track them down and bring them to justice.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe chase runs along the coastal road to an unfinished viewpoint turnout above the cliffs. Beyond the turnout the road continues toward a busier settlement, but the target's planned road escape ends at the viewpoint. A concrete drainage path descends from the viewpoint to a cave entrance halfway down the slope. The cave has a broad dry chamber, a narrow smoke-trapping side recess, and one lower opening onto a small isolated rock ledge cut off by sheer rock on both sides; the ledge cannot be reached safely while smoke fills the chamber. The target's car carries an accelerant canister in the boot solely for destroying evidence; it is not part of any improvised explosive device.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe target drives alone. A second criminal waits nowhere nearby; this is a solo evidence-destruction plan. Two picnickers occupy the far edge of the viewpoint and will flee from obvious danger. A municipal maintenance worker is driving uphill on the same coastal road from farther down-route and becomes relevant only if smoke or a crash blocks the road.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success means the target is apprehended without catastrophic civilian harm; preserving the vehicle's documentary evidence materially strengthens the case but is not required. Partial success means decisive documents are preserved while the target escapes beyond credible recovery. Failure means the target escapes and the vehicle evidence is destroyed beyond useful recovery, the player can no longer continue, or the pursuit causes catastrophic civilian harm.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack the target's distance to the viewpoint, the car's condition, custody of the document envelope in the glove box, ignition of the deliberate vehicle fire, smoke at the cave entrance, and the target's foot lead. The target sets the car alight only after reaching the viewpoint and removing one personal bag. Once fire starts, documents deteriorate from heat over concrete time and smoke is carried downslope toward the cave entrance.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe target knows arrest is likely if the car is examined, because the glove box contains signed invoices and a second set of registration papers. The escape plan is to reach the viewpoint, ignite the interior, and descend to the cave while responders focus on the burning vehicle.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The target begins alone in the car.\n- A document envelope in the glove box contains the strongest physical case evidence and remains there unless someone removes it.\n- The target carries a small personal bag out of the car but does not take the document envelope.\n- The accelerant canister is in the boot and is used only at the viewpoint after the target abandons the vehicle.\n- The cave is the target's hiding place; smoke can make its upper entrance dangerous but does not create a new exit.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nApprehend the target. Decide how much risk and time to spend preserving the vehicle evidence once the target abandons it. Securing documents without capture is only partial success; pursuing the target while the evidence burns can still produce full mission success if the arrest objective is completed.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nBefore the viewpoint, the target stops trying to widen the gap and instead repeatedly checks the mirror and glove-box area, signaling that the planned endpoint matters more than pure escape speed.\n\nLive danger:\nThe deliberate car fire creates a real choice between immediate foot pursuit and evidence preservation, with smoke and civilians at the viewpoint.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The personal bag the target takes from the car contains clothes, cash, and a charger; the key case documents remain in the glove box.\n- The unfinished viewpoint road does not continue into a hidden mountain route.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe target is still two bends from the viewpoint. The document envelope is intact in the glove box, the accelerant remains in the boot, and the picnickers are already at the viewpoint.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the target ignites the car, the document envelope remains recoverable for a short physical window before heat ruins it; the target simultaneously gains distance down the drainage path.\n- If the picnickers are not driven away after ignition, they retreat toward the road and can obstruct vehicle movement but do not become hostages.\n- If smoke blocks the upper cave mouth before the target reaches it, the target stays on the slope and seeks cover rather than acquiring an impossible alternate entrance.",
