      "MISSION 1: Retro Rumble - Egaleo vs. Haidari\n  Category: Racing & Driving\n\n  Public description to show in mission selection:\n  Embed yourself within an 80s-styled motorcycle gang in Egaleo, known for their mullets, leather jackets, flowery shirts, and big red Yamahas. Engage in their escalating turf war against a rival 2000s-era hipster gang based in Haidari. Execute missions for the bikers, participate in street clashes, but remain aware that opportunities may arise to switch allegiances and betray your initial employers.\n\n  PRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\n  Fixed spatial layout:\n  A late-night food-and-motor meet occupies a broad parking lot between ordinary commercial strips. Egaleo riders use the west entrance and a row of red Yamahas as their visible claim; Haidari riders use the east entrance beside a temporary espresso stand. A neutral organizer works from a cash van near the center, while a service lane behind the stalls reaches both exits without crossing the crowd. Each side still has its home base, but the live mission is concentrated on the market lot, cash van, service lane and the two entrances.\n\n  Standing roles, positions, and routines:\n  The Egaleo treasurer commands the player's loyalty errand and holds the gang's vendor-collection envelope. The neutral organizer keeps duplicate numbered receipts in the cash van. A Haidari photographer handles their event page and has images showing the treasurer collecting money from vendors on dates he blamed on Haidari. Ordinary vendors and customers remain civilians, not gang assets. Both gang leaders stay outside the densest crowd unless violence begins.\n\n  Resolution boundaries:\n  For Egaleo loyalty, full success means completing the assigned collection/territory job while keeping the market under Egaleo control and resolving the missing-money accusation without exposing the player as a thief. For defection, full success means transferring documentary proof of the treasurer's frame-up to Haidari — the organizer's duplicate receipts, the photographer's timestamped images, or both — without the market collapsing into uncontrolled civilian-endangering violence. The collection money can be additional leverage but is not proof by itself. Partial success wins the lot but leaves the theft unresolved, or proves the theft only after civilian violence destroys the market. Failure loses the meaningful money/evidence leverage and leaves both gangs blaming the player for the night's collapse.\n\n  Pressure and continuity:\n  Track the market opening, custody of the collection envelope, crowd density and which riders control each entrance. After the final vendor settlement, the organizer closes the cash van and drives it through the rear service lane to the west entrance, taking the duplicate receipt book with him unless its custody has already changed. Once a fight begins in the central lot, ordinary people flood toward the two public exits and motorcycle movement becomes much more dangerous.\n\n  FIXED MISSION FACTS\n\n  Prepared situation:\n  The turf war has been fed by missing vendor deposits. Haidari did not steal them. The Egaleo treasurer has been skimming cash for months and blaming the rivals to keep his standing. Tonight he gives the player the collection envelope and orders a humiliating collection from vendors near the Haidari entrance, expecting any resistance to justify a show of force.\n\n  Objective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n  - The Egaleo collection envelope begins in the treasurer's jacket and transfers to the player only when he assigns the errand.\n- The organizer's duplicate receipt book stays inside the locked cash van until final settlement; the organizer holds the van key.\n- The Haidari photographer's phone contains timestamped images of the treasurer collecting from the same vendors on prior nights.\n- The treasurer has no control over the organizer or the photographer; he relies on gang intimidation to keep the accounts from being compared.\n\n  What must be accomplished:\n  Complete or betray the night's territorial collection job with actual control of the money or proof that explains the feud. The player is free to bargain, steal, expose, intimidate or switch sides, but civilian harm and lost custody remain real consequences.\n\n  Useful lead or workable leverage:\n  Two vendors independently mention that their old receipts carry the Egaleo treasurer's initials even though he now claims Haidari collected those nights.\n\n  Live danger:\n  A ring of motorcycles can turn a comic territorial display into a crush hazard and close both exits around civilians.\n\n  Misleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n  - A Haidari rider carrying a thick cash roll is paying stall deposits for the espresso stand.\n- A vendor's missing lockbox was misplaced before the gangs arrived.\n\n  Binding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\n  The market is opening and crowd density is still low. The organizer is inside the cash van checking yesterday's duplicate receipts; the Egaleo treasurer still holds tonight's collection envelope. The Haidari photographer is at the east entrance taking ordinary event photographs and still carries the phone containing the older timestamped images.\n\n  Later consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n  - When final settlement begins, the organizer takes the duplicate receipt book out of the van and keeps it in hand until departure.\n- If the collection envelope disappears without an explanation, the Egaleo treasurer names the player as the thief before either leader hears another account.",
