      "MISSION 1: Surveil a Rock Bar in Nikaia\nCategory: Surveillance & Stakeouts\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nGather evidence on an arms deal taking place in a notorious rock bar.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe bar has a long counter, small pool-table room, basement beer cellar and rear delivery stair. The cellar reaches a delivery shutter opening onto a vehicle-passable alley that returns to the public street. A tiny office above the cellar stair overlooks only the counter area. The buyer's light van is parked near the alley mouth. The pool room has no hidden exit.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe bar owner acts as broker and receives the buyer's cash at the counter. The actual seller waits in the upstairs office with a numbered claim ticket. A cellar worker who normally handles kegs has been paid to keep one sealed crate untouched. The buyer remains in the pool room until closing and holds the light van's ignition key.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe weapons are already inside the sealed crate in the beer cellar. The buyer pays the owner during normal bar service and receives the matching half of the claim ticket. At closing, the seller comes downstairs, matches the ticket halves, then the seller and buyer move the sealed crate through the delivery shutter and along the alley to the buyer's light van. The cellar worker does not know what is inside.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success links the payment, matching claim ticket and cellar crate to the buyer, broker and seller. Securing the crate with those identities documented also succeeds. Partial success finds or secures the crate without proving the financial handoff or all three roles. Failure lets the crate reach the buyer's van and that vehicle get beyond practical interception before a usable transaction chain or the weapons are preserved.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack buyer payment, ticket custody, cellar-worker routine, owner awareness and closing-time shutter access. Normal bar receipts remain separate from the off-book envelope.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe sealed crate has been in the cellar since the afternoon delivery. The owner is running a normal busy bar; once payment is accepted the off-book envelope remains under the owner's control. The seller stays upstairs and does not need to appear publicly before closing. The buyer's light van is already parked near the alley mouth.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The arms shipment starts in the sealed cellar crate.\n- The owner receives the buyer's cash envelope at the counter.\n- The seller holds one half of a numbered claim ticket; the buyer receives the matching half after payment.\n- The buyer holds the ignition key to the light van parked near the alley mouth; the alley is the crate's only vehicle-removal route.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nGather a defensible evidence chain linking payment to the hidden arms crate and the three deal participants, or disrupt the release while preserving those links.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nA crate recorded as empty keg returns has a fresh private seal and is never moved with the other cellar stock.\n\nLive danger:\nClosing time brings the seller downstairs and starts the crate's alley transfer toward the buyer's van.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nA cellar worker makes one ordinary keg run before closing and walks past the sealed crate without touching it.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If payment has not yet been accepted at mission start, the owner gives the buyer the matching claim-ticket half once the payment is accepted.\n- At closing, the seller comes down from the office and checks both ticket halves.\n- After the match, the seller and buyer move the cellar crate through the rear shutter and along the alley to the buyer's light van.\n- Once the crate is loaded, the buyer drives the van along the alley to the public street; the crate is externally lost only if the van gets beyond practical interception.",
