      "MISSION 1: Varkiza Hotel Arms Deal\nCategory: Surveillance & Stakeouts\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nIntelligence reports an illegal arms deal going down at a luxurious Varkiza\nhotel. Conduct surveillance, identify the key players, and gather evidence.\nDecide whether to simply report your findings or intervene to disrupt the\nexchange and secure the weapons.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe hotel's sea terrace steps down to a private beach deck with several locked cabanas. The lobby and terrace are linked by a glass corridor; a service stair descends from the terrace kitchen to the beach deck. One cabana at the far end is rented under the seller's alias. A small tender waits offshore beyond the hotel swimming area; at the far end of the deck a short service landing allows the tender to nose alongside. The buyer's car remains at the main entrance.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe buyer sits at a terrace table with one associate and holds the ignition key to the car at the main entrance. The seller circulates between lobby and terrace. A beach attendant holds the master cabana keys but is uninvolved. The seller's beach courier occupies the rented cabana with the sample and holds that cabana's working key. A boatman offshore is hired only to remove the seller and case if the meeting collapses.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe visible terrace meeting handles identity and price; the actual weapon sample remains locked in the beach cabana until the buyer agrees to inspect it. The seller then takes the buyer down the service stair while the associate stays on the terrace. After inspection, the case returns to the cabana. On the normal route, payment confirmation triggers the beach courier to carry the case up the service stair to the terrace, where the buyer receives it and takes it through the glass corridor and lobby to the main-entrance car. The offshore tender is an emergency seller exit: on the seller's alarm, it approaches the service landing and the courier carries the case from the cabana to that landing for physical boarding.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nSurveillance is a Full success once the terrace principals are tied to the cabana sample and the inspection/payment movement is documented with reliable continuity; later road or sea departure does not erase that completed intelligence picture. Intervention is a Full success if the weapon is secured and external road/sea removal is prevented while principal identities remain provable. Partial success secures the sample but loses a principal through the alternate exit, or documents the meeting without ever confirming the weapon. Failure occurs if neither a complete surveillance chain nor control of the sample exists before the case leaves by buyer car or tender beyond practical interception.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack terrace negotiation, cabana-key custody, buyer movement, sample-case custody, beach-attendant routine and tender readiness. The tender approaches the deck only after the seller sends the emergency signal; it does not hover at the shore from the start.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nNegotiation is underway on the terrace. The real sample is in a locked case inside the far cabana with the beach courier. The seller does not possess the cabana key; the courier does. The buyer retains the ignition key to the car at the main entrance. The tender waits offshore at a fixed distance with engine off.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The arms sample starts inside the far rented cabana under the beach courier's control.\n- The buyer starts on the terrace and reaches the cabana only after agreeing to inspect.\n- The buyer controls the main-entrance car key and is the normal road-removal driver.\n- The offshore tender remains stationary until the seller sends the emergency signal.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nEstablish that the terrace negotiation is tied to the weapon in the hotel cabana, then report the deal or interrupt the exchange before the sample leaves by road or sea.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe seller repeatedly checks the service-stair landing rather than the hotel entrance, and the far cabana remains locked despite the beach deck otherwise winding down.\n\nLive danger:\nThe hotel offers two physically separate exits once the seller becomes alarmed: the buyer's road car and the seller's emergency tender.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- Other locked cabanas contain ordinary guest property and towels.\n- A yacht visible farther offshore is unrelated to the deal.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe beach attendant makes one final towel collection along the deck before returning to the service stair. The far cabana remains under the courier's control throughout.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- When the buyer agrees to inspect, the seller leads the buyer down the service stair and the courier unlocks the far cabana from inside.\n- After inspection, the courier returns the case to the cabana unless an intervention changes custody.\n- On normal payment confirmation, the courier carries the case up the service stair to the terrace, physically hands it to the buyer, and the buyer takes it through the glass corridor/lobby to the main-entrance car before driving onto the coastal road.\n- If the seller sends the emergency signal while the sample remains under seller/courier control, the offshore tender starts its engine and approaches the service landing; the courier carries the case from the cabana to that landing, where the seller and case can board. If the sample has already left seller/courier custody, the tender can remove the seller but does not magically recover the case.\n- External case loss exists only once the buyer car or tender gets beyond practical interception with the sample; a completed surveillance chain remains completed.\n\n",
