      "MISSION 1: Varkiza Hotel Arms Deal\nCategory: Surveillance & Stakeouts\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nIntelligence reports an illegal arms deal going down at a luxurious Varkiza\nhotel. Conduct surveillance, identify the key players, and gather evidence.\nDecide whether to simply report your findings or intervene to disrupt the\nexchange and secure the weapons.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\nFixed spatial layout:\nThe main deal uses the hotel's lounge and underground garage. The lobby opens into a quiet sea-view lounge where the principals meet; a guest elevator reaches the garage, while a separate vehicle ramp opens onto the coastal road. The seller's SUV is parked in a reserved garage bay with the main weapon crate locked in the rear. The buyer's dark sedan waits in a nearby garage bay under the buyer's driver. A small inspection sample is carried upstairs in a slim case.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe seller meets the buyer in the lounge with one bodyguard. The buyer arrives with two armed associates and a driver; the driver waits in the dark sedan in the garage and holds its ignition key. The seller's second guard remains beside the seller SUV, holds that SUV's ignition key and can drive it if ordered. Hotel security patrols lobby and garage on routine rounds. The buyer has privately instructed the associates to seize the larger crate after confirming the sample, without completing payment.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe arms deal is genuine, but the buyer intends a robbery immediately after inspection. The slim sample case goes from garage to lounge; after approval, seller and buyer are supposed to descend together for the larger crate and payment. Instead, the buyer's associates plan to isolate the seller in the garage, force or disarm the second guard, open the seller SUV, remove the larger crate and carry it to the buyer sedan. The buyer driver then has the established vehicle route out through the garage ramp. The seller does not know this. Surveillance can document both the deal and the impending betrayal; intervention can occur before or during the split.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nSurveillance is a Full success once both organizations, the inspection sample, the larger garage crate and the buyer's planned seizure are linked with reliable continuity; later flight does not erase that completed intelligence picture. Intervention is a Full success if the arms transfer and robbery are both stopped and the weapons are secured without losing the identity/custody chain. Partial success stops the robbery but leaves the wider original arms network unproved, or secures only the inspection sample while the larger crate remains outside player control. Failure occurs only if neither a complete surveillance picture nor control of the main weapons exists before the larger crate leaves beyond practical interception in either the warned seller SUV or the buyer sedan after the double-cross.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack sample-case custody, lounge negotiation, buyer-associate positioning, garage security patrol and seller-SUV readiness. The double-cross begins only after the buyer physically verifies the sample and gives the agreed phrase to the associates.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe seller and buyer are meeting in the lounge. The slim sample case is under the seller's chair. The larger crate remains locked in the seller SUV in the garage under the second guard, who holds the SUV ignition key. The buyer's driver waits in the nearby dark sedan with its ignition key. The buyer's two associates are separated: one in the lobby, one already near the garage elevator.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The inspection sample starts in the slim case under the seller's control in the lounge.\n- The larger weapons crate starts locked inside the seller SUV in the reserved garage bay.\n- The seller's second guard holds the seller-SUV ignition key.\n- The buyer's driver waits in the buyer sedan in the garage and holds its ignition key.\n- The buyer's associates begin in separate hotel positions and act against the seller only after the buyer's agreed phrase.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nIdentify the genuine arms deal and its participants, then report the complete picture or stop both the transfer and the buyer's planned seizure without losing the weapon custody chain.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe buyer's associates watch the seller's movements more than hotel exits, and one has positioned near the garage elevator despite the negotiation taking place upstairs.\n\nLive danger:\nOnce the buyer triggers the robbery, two armed groups with conflicting plans converge in the enclosed garage around the larger weapon crate.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- The seller's SUV contains ordinary luggage beside the locked weapon crate.\n- Hotel security has noticed neither group and is following its normal patrol.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe garage security patrol is on the opposite ramp when the lounge inspection begins and returns toward the reserved bays on its normal circuit.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- After the buyer verifies the inspection sample, the agreed phrase activates the two associates' move toward the seller and garage.\n- If the seller receives warning before descending, the second guard starts the seller SUV. On direct instruction or visible attack, the guard can drive the SUV—with the larger crate still inside—out through the garage ramp onto the coastal road.\n- If the buyer's associates gain physical control of the larger crate first, they carry it from the seller SUV to the nearby buyer sedan.\n- Once the crate is loaded into the buyer sedan, the buyer driver can leave by the garage ramp onto the coastal road.\n- External main-crate loss exists only once whichever vehicle actually carries the crate gets beyond practical interception; a surveillance picture already completed before that departure remains completed.\n\n",
