      "MISSION 1: Argyroupoli Car Theft Ring\n  Category: Investigations\n\n  Public description to show in mission selection:\n  Disrupt a sophisticated car theft ring operating out of Argyroupoli. Track stolen vehicles and gather evidence.\n\n  PRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\n  Fixed spatial layout:\n  The operation has no public criminal storefront. A stolen hatchback moves from a residential side street to a multilevel apartment garage whose lower level blocks ordinary tracker reception. A narrow exit ramp reaches a second street; a pedestrian stair and lift reach the apartment lobby. A parked electronics van on the upper ramp operates the jammer. A locked wire storage cage on the lower level contains plates and diagnostic records, while the buyer's meeting point is a petrol-station forecourt several blocks away.\n\n  Standing roles, positions, and routines:\n  A two-person theft crew drives the stolen car; the crew member responsible for plate changes carries the storage-cage key. A jammer operator remains in the electronics van; a garage custodian lets vehicles through the lower gate in exchange for cash. The buyer stays away from the garage and comes to the petrol-station forecourt only after receiving a clean-plate photograph. Residents use the garage normally and do not know the scheme.\n\n  Resolution boundaries:\n  Full success identifies the deliberate dead-zone mechanism, preserves evidence connecting stolen car, jammer crew and plate store, and secures the car or documents the buyer acceptance/handoff. Partial success recovers the car without proving the network, or proves the jammer/plate network after the car gets away. Failure requires the hatchback to get beyond practical interception after the identity change while no sufficient dead-zone/plate/network link has been preserved.\n\n  Pressure and continuity:\n  Track tracker signal, car position, jammer state, lower-gate access and the clean-plate photograph. Signal loss begins only when the car crosses the lower ramp. The crew changes plates beside the storage cage; only after a photograph of the new plate is sent does the buyer travel to the petrol station. The hatchback leaves the garage by the second-street ramp and continues to that forecourt with one thief driving. Disabling the jammer restores reception but also tells its operator that surveillance is present.\n\n  Prepared situation:\n  Repeated GPS dropouts in earlier thefts were not urban interference: the ring uses one residential underground garage as a controlled blind pocket. Tonight a victim's hatchback still carries a hidden insurer tracker, giving the player one chance to reconstruct the dead-zone system before the crew changes its identity.\n\n  Objective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n  - The stolen hatchback begins on the residential side street and still carries the insurer tracker behind the rear trim.\n- The jammer unit is bolted beneath a workbench inside the electronics van on the upper ramp; its operator has the remote power control.\n- A storage cage on lower level -2 holds four cloned plates, a handheld diagnostic tablet and a notebook matching stolen VINs to temporary plates.\n- The garage custodian holds the lower-gate fob. The buyer does not enter the garage tonight.\n\n  What must be accomplished:\n  Follow or intercept the stolen vehicle without reducing the case to a single recovery: establish how the deliberate dead zone and identity change connect to the ring, and secure the car or decisive network evidence before the handoff completes.\n\n  Useful lead or workable leverage:\n  The tracker's last three historical dropouts all form the same sharp rectangle on the insurer's map rather than fading gradually along a road.\n\n  Live danger:\n  A blind pursuit down the garage ramp can put the player nose-to-nose with the theft crew while the jammer still prevents outside tracking or easy coordination.\n\n  Misleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n  - A second car with a faulty aftermarket tracker loses signal elsewhere for innocent reasons.\n- The garage custodian's cash envelope includes legitimate residents' maintenance fees as well as the bribe.\n\n  Binding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\n  The stolen hatchback is approaching the garage while its tracker still reports normally. The jammer van is already parked on the upper ramp with its side door shut; the custodian is inside the glass booth watching resident traffic. The storage cage remains locked on level -2.\n\n  Later consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n  - Once the clean-plate photograph is sent, the buyer comes to the petrol-station forecourt by ordinary taxi and waits for the hatchback or an abort message. If the hatchback arrives, the buyer inspects it and leaves as a passenger while one thief drives the stolen car onward; the second thief separates from them.\n  - If the theft crew realizes the tracker survived, they remove it and drop it in the garage stairwell; the car itself continues on the established exit route.\n\n  ",
