      "MISSION 1: Argyroupoli Car Theft Ring\n  Category: Investigations\n\n  Public description to show in mission selection:\n  Disrupt a sophisticated car theft ring operating out of Argyroupoli. Track stolen vehicles and gather evidence.\n\n  PRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\n  Fixed spatial layout:\n  A collision-repair shop occupies the ground floor of a small industrial building. Its paint booth connects to a rear fenced apron where a legal salvage wreck and one stolen luxury car stand under covers. An insurance assessor rents a tiny office across the courtyard; a vehicle transporter can enter only through the apron gate. A parts mezzanine contains removed VIN plates and airbags. The public reception cannot see the rear apron.\n\n  Standing roles, positions, and routines:\n  A bodywork specialist physically transfers identity parts; the shop owner handles stolen-car intake; a corrupt insurance assessor supplies paperwork from written-off donor cars; one transporter driver is due to collect a finished clone. Two legitimate mechanics work only in the front bays and believe the rear job is an insurance rebuild.\n\n  Resolution boundaries:\n  Full success preserves a matched set: donor-wreck identity evidence, the cloned stolen vehicle, and paperwork or testimony linking the assessor to the transfer, while securing the clone or maintaining a reliable track on the departing transporter. Partial success secures only one side of that matched chain, or preserves the donor/assessor proof after the clone departs. Failure requires the finished clone to leave beyond practical interception with plausible papers while the remaining identity evidence is too damaged or incomplete to establish a matched donor-to-stolen-vehicle chain.\n\n  Pressure and continuity:\n  Track the stolen car's original VIN marks, the donor wreck, removed identity pieces, assessor paperwork and transporter arrival. The specialist grinds the final original stamping only after the assessor signs the donor release. The transporter can load the clone only after the new plates and signed release sheet are both present; the driver uses the transporter's own winch and loading gear to bring the clone aboard.\n\n  Prepared situation:\n  This ring does not mainly chop cars. It gives high-value stolen vehicles the legal identity of genuine write-offs. Tonight's silver coupe is almost finished; its donor is a fire-damaged wreck of the same model sitting ten metres away, creating unusually strong physical evidence if both are secured before separation.\n\n  Objective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n  - The stolen silver coupe is under the rear-apron cover with its original secondary VIN still visible beneath the passenger seat rail.\n- The fire-damaged donor wreck sits beside it; its front identity plate has already been removed.\n- The removed donor plate and two earlier plates are in a magnetic tray on the parts mezzanine.\n- The assessor holds the unsigned donor-release sheet in his courtyard office; his laptop contains photographs of three earlier donor/stolen pairings.\n\n  What must be accomplished:\n  Gather matched evidence that proves stolen vehicles are being reborn under donor identities, and secure the current stolen coupe or prevent its legal-looking transfer. A shop full of suspicious parts is not enough without a matched identity chain.\n\n  Useful lead or workable leverage:\n  The donor wreck's auction lot number appears handwritten on a clean new windscreen tag attached to the intact silver coupe.\n\n  Live danger:\n  If the shop believes police or insurers are coming, the bodywork specialist's first priority is destroying removable VIN plates and grinding the coupe's remaining original stamp.\n\n  Misleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n  - A bucket of loose number plates belongs to customer crash repairs and is logged normally.\n- The legitimate mechanics' cash-in-hand overtime is tax evasion but unrelated to the theft ring.\n\n  Binding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\n  The assessor is still in his office arguing with the shop owner about payment; the donor-release sheet is unsigned. The stolen coupe remains covered and the bodywork specialist is fitting the donor's airbag module.\n\n  Later consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n  - Once the signed donor release reaches the shop floor, the owner calls the transporter if it is not already waiting outside. When the transporter is present and both new plates and signed release are available, the driver winches the clone onto the transporter and leaves through the apron gate.\n  - If the ring receives a concrete warning before the loose identity pieces are secured, the bodywork specialist takes the magnetic tray into the paint-booth work area and destroys the loose donor plates with ordinary shop tools. If the original secondary VIN is also ground away, the strongest direct donor-to-clone match is permanently weakened, though other evidence can still survive.\n\n  ",
