      "MISSION 1: Argyroupoli Car Theft Ring\n  Category: Investigations\n\n  Public description to show in mission selection:\n  Disrupt a sophisticated car theft ring operating out of Argyroupoli. Track stolen vehicles and gather evidence.\n\n  PRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\n  Fixed spatial layout:\n  A glossy used-car photo studio and detailing hall fronts the ring. Behind the cyclorama wall, a vehicle door reaches a secure loading court where a two-level car carrier can take four vehicles. A glass sales office holds export paperwork; an internal basement stair beside the office corridor reaches a separate locked cage containing original number plates and owners' documents. The carrier exits onto the main road and does not return once loaded. The showroom frontage opens onto the same main-road pavement. There is no underground blind garage or fixer office elsewhere.\n\n  Standing roles, positions, and routines:\n  The front's sales manager creates fake export listings and carries the basement-cage keycard; a photographer records clean catalogue images; two drivers shuttle stolen cars into the studio; the carrier driver is a paid transporter who suspects the cars are dirty but does not know individual thefts. A document broker remains in the glass office until the carrier leaves.\n\n  Resolution boundaries:\n  Full success stops or tracks the export movement while preserving evidence matching at least two stolen cars to falsified listings or original-owner documents. Partial success saves one car or captures the carrier but loses the documentary chain, or preserves a solid two-car export/document link after the vehicles depart. Failure requires the identified cars and the remaining documentary/original-owner evidence to leave beyond practical recovery before any reliable two-car export chain has been preserved.\n\n  Pressure and continuity:\n  Track which cars are photographed, which are loaded, custody of export packets, custody of the basement originals and the carrier's finite departure condition. A car is loaded only after its catalogue photo and export packet are complete. The carrier departs when three assigned cars are aboard or when the manager receives a concrete alarm, whichever happens first. On an alarm, the manager leaves the sales floor for the basement cage while the broker gathers the remaining export packets in the glass office; only after those separate tasks are completed do they head toward the showroom frontage.\n\n  Prepared situation:\n  Tonight is consolidation night. Rather than dismantling the stolen cars, the ring photographs them as clean dealer stock and sends them out together under fabricated export identities. Two cars already match recent theft reports; a third is due from a street driver, giving the player a chance to connect moving vehicle, paperwork and front company in one place.\n\n  Objective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n  - Two identified stolen cars begin in the detailing hall; neither is yet on the carrier.\n- Each export packet begins in the glass sales office and is matched to a fabricated online listing on the manager's computer.\n- The basement cage contains the two cars' original number plates and one owner's service wallet.\n- The carrier driver holds only the transport manifest; it becomes incriminating when matched to the false export packets and identified vehicles.\n\n  What must be accomplished:\n  Track the stolen vehicles into the export front, preserve the document/identity link, and prevent the identified cars from disappearing in the carrier or retain enough concrete evidence to prove the export chain after departure.\n\n  Useful lead or workable leverage:\n  A supposedly dealer-owned car in the online listing still has a distinctive child-seat anchor cover visible in a victim's theft photograph.\n\n  Live danger:\n  Once the carrier moves, recovering the cars becomes a live road pursuit with a heavy vehicle and civilian traffic rather than a static search.\n\n  Misleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n  - Several expensive cars in the front lot are genuinely consigned for sale.\n- The photographer's retouching of scratches is deceptive advertising but not itself evidence of theft.\n\n  Binding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\n  Two stolen cars are in the detailing hall; the first has been photographed, the second has not. The carrier is empty in the loading court and the document broker is building the first export packet in the sales office. The basement originals remain locked in the cage under the manager's keycard control.\n\n  Later consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n  - After three assigned cars are loaded, the document broker gives the carrier driver the final manifest and the carrier departs onto the main road; the basement originals remain in the cage unless another event moves them.\n  - If the manager receives a concrete alarm before three cars are loaded, he orders immediate carrier departure with whatever assigned cars are already aboard. In parallel, the document broker gathers the remaining export packets and heads out through the showroom frontage, while the manager goes to the basement cage, places the original plates and service wallet in a document satchel and then returns toward the same frontage. Those records are not externally lost until their holders get beyond practical follow-up on the public road.\n\n  ",
