      "MISSION 1: Lost Villages of Hymettus\nCategory: Investigations\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nConnect with a journalist who believes she can find the semi-mythical lost villages\nhidden in the gorges above Terpsithea or on the plateaus over Argyroupoli. Gain her\ntrust, escort her from her residence, and protect her as she investigates these\nforgotten places. Be prepared for unknown dangers, from wary, isolated inhabitants to\nsomething far older and more unsettling that dwells in the mountain's heart.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nExpedition graph: journalist pickup -> city-edge trailhead -> mountain fork. The goat branch runs through the dumping landmark and narrow traverse to the old water source and shared water crossing. The plateau branch climbs past the shepherd wall, crosses above that water point, and ends at the roofless chapel ruin. The gorge descent leaves the crossing toward a lower exit; its side spur ends at a rockfall. A higher saddle behind the chapel returns to the plateau branch, not to the city.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nThe journalist begins at pickup with independent motives; ordinary hikers and occasional dumpers remain on the trailhead and lower branches. The uncanny acoustic presence remains anchored inside the roofless chapel ruin; no isolated inhabitants or human watcher exists. Any pursuit must enter through the trailhead or higher saddle and follow one of those established approaches.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success: the journalist reaches and credibly documents the reality's true site and both return safely. Partial: the true site is identified or documented, but evidence, trust, or safe return is compromised. Failure: the journalist is lost or incapacitated, the real site remains unproved, or an actual physical hazard leaves no established return route safely usable before extraction.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack daylight and weather, water, journalist trust and condition, trail knowledge, evidence\nintegrity, and uncanny effects tied to fixed landmarks.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe uncanny ruin misdirects through repeated sound rather than moving terrain. Water, loose stones, and dehydration remain the practical threats. Chasing an echo away from the fixed path can pull the pair out of sight of the known descent line, but the route itself remains where the landmarks place it and can be recovered.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The true site is the roofless chapel ruin at the end of the plateau branch above the water crossing.\n- Its recorded acoustic repetition and identifiable ruins constitute credible documentation; returning safely matters more than explaining the phenomenon completely.\n- The repeated sound remains anchored to the chapel ruin and alters perception of direction without moving the physical path.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nThe journalist reaches and documents the true site and both return with credible evidence.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nRepeated echo from wrong direction.\n\nLive danger:\nDehydration and loose stones.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe roofless chapel becomes visible only after the plateau branch rises above the water crossing. Its repeated sound carries down toward the fork, making acoustic direction less reliable than the fixed landmarks.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the player leaves the fixed path to chase the echo, they can lose visual contact with the known descent line and must recover it from the water crossing, wall-lines, slope and other fixed landmarks; the physical route does not move.\n\nOperational facts:\n- The plateau branch crosses the water source and ends at the roofless chapel ruin.\n- The uncanny effect is confined to sound and perception around the chapel; the physical route, site and people remain where they are.",
