      "MISSION 1: Zografou University Hack\nCategory: Cyber & High-Tech Operations\n\nPublic description to show in mission selection:\nPrevent a cyber attack on the university, track the hackers, and secure the systems. This will take place inside the campus.\n\nPRIVATE GM DOSSIER (never reveal to the player)\n\nFixed spatial layout:\nCampus gate -> administration building lobby -> network operations corridor -> NOC room and telecom room. A helpdesk office opens from the same corridor, while a staff stair reaches the roof equipment level. The telecom room holds the core firewall console and a beige Windows 95 workstation connected as a serial-console/staging terminal; the NOC room holds monitoring terminals. Public campus spaces do not connect directly to either controlled room.\n\nStanding roles, positions, and routines:\nA senior network administrator is the primary insider attacker and has legitimate access to the telecom room. He has stubbornly kept the Windows 95 console workstation in service for years because he prefers its old terminal workflow and serial tools. A junior colleague covers the action by filing false outage tickets from the helpdesk office. Another NOC operator is on duty and believes the maintenance is legitimate. Campus security controls the administration lobby but does not understand the network change.\n\nPersistent causal rules:\nThe senior administrator has used the Windows 95 console workstation to stage and issue a malicious firewall rule that opens an external path to an exam-bank repository; a pending configuration reload would copy that rule into the saved firewall state. The workstation's local staging file and terminal history preserve a second technical trail to the change. The junior colleague's false outage tickets disguise the traffic change. Securing the university requires removing the malicious rule from live and saved configuration, revoking the senior administrator's privileged account, revoking the junior account used for the cover operation and preserving enough of the change log, staging trail and ticket trail to identify both insiders.\n\nResolution boundaries:\nFull success blocks the external path, removes the malicious rule from live and saved configuration, revokes both insider accounts and preserves evidence linking the administrator and junior colleague. Partial success fully secures the technical path but loses one insider's attribution or part of the ticket/staging evidence. Failure leaves the external path active, leaves the malicious saved configuration able to return on reload, or leaves the senior administrator with valid privileged access capable of recreating it.\n\nPressure and continuity:\nTrack firewall reload state, live versus saved rule state, privileged-account sessions, false ticket creation, NOC operator awareness and physical access to the telecom-room Windows 95 console and helpdesk office.\n\nFIXED MISSION FACTS\n\nPrepared situation:\nThe cyber attack is disguised as legitimate network maintenance. The malicious route is already open. The senior administrator has staged the change through his personally preserved Windows 95 console workstation, but the reload that would save the rule permanently has not yet completed.\n\nObjective, evidence, custody, and decisive placement:\n- The senior administrator created the malicious firewall rule from the telecom-room Windows 95 console workstation.\n- Its local staging file and terminal history preserve a technical link to the rule.\n- The junior colleague is generating false outage tickets from the helpdesk office to conceal the change.\n- The pending configuration reload would make the malicious route survive an ordinary restart unless the saved configuration is cleaned.\n\nWhat must be accomplished:\nClose the malicious external path, prevent it becoming persistent, revoke both insider accounts and preserve the technical trail identifying the attackers.\n\nUseful lead or workable leverage:\nThe firewall change log conflicts with the junior colleague's unrelated wireless-outage ticket; the senior administrator's unusual Windows 95 staging workstation can preserve an independent local history of the same change.\n\nLive danger:\nThe pending configuration reload converts a removable live rule into the saved firewall state.\n\nMisleading, partial, or irrelevant elements:\n- A genuine wireless outage in another campus building exists but is unrelated to the exam-bank intrusion.\n\nBinding spatial, custody, and parallel conditions:\nThe on-duty NOC operator leaves the monitoring desk to check a roof-equipment alarm while the senior administrator remains in the telecom room and the junior colleague stays at helpdesk.\n\nLater consequences; apply only when their cause occurs:\n- If the configuration reload completes, the malicious rule enters the saved state and must be removed explicitly from both live and stored configuration; reload completion alone does not make later remediation impossible.\n- Revoking only the senior administrator stops his privileged reuse but leaves the junior colleague's account and cover trail unresolved.\n- Once the rule is removed from live and stored configuration and both accounts are revoked, this insider path is secured.",
